2. What types of blocks are there in Java?

There are 3 main types of blocks you should know:

1. Instance Initialization Block

class Test {


    {
        System.out.println("Instance block");
    }
}

Runs when an object is created.
----
2. Static Initialization Block

class Test {


    static {
        System.out.println("Static block");
    }
}

Runs when the class is loaded.
----
3. Synchronized Block

class Test {


    void show() {


        synchronized (Test.class) {
            System.out.println("Synchronized");
        }
    }
}

Used for thread synchronization.
It must be inside executable code.
------------------------------------------ synchronized(ClassName.class) ------------------
class level lock : Even though multiple objects have their own object locks, ClassName.class provides one common class-level lock that all those objects can use for synchronization.

class Counter {

    static int count = 0;

    void increment() {

        synchronized (Counter.class) {
            count++;
        }
    }
}

You created:

Counter c1 = new Counter();
Counter c2 = new Counter();

There are two objects:

c1 → 🔒 object lock
c2 → 🔒 object lock

But Counter.class is one thing:

Counter.class → 🔒 ONE class-level lock

So:

T1 → c1 → Counter.class 🔒
T2 → c2 → Counter.class 🔒

Both threads want the same lock.
Therefore, one waits.
------------------------------------- synchronized(this) ------------------------------
class Counter {

    void increment() {

        synchronized(this) {
            // critical section
        }
    }
}

Create 3 objects:

Counter c1 = new Counter();
Counter c2 = new Counter();
Counter c3 = new Counter();

Each object has its own lock:

c1 → 🔒 Lock 1
c2 → 🔒 Lock 2
c3 → 🔒 Lock 3

When you write:

synchronized(this)

this means the current object.

So:

T1 → c1 → this = c1 → 🔒 Lock 1
T2 → c2 → this = c2 → 🔒 Lock 2
T3 → c3 → this = c3 → 🔒 Lock 3

Therefore, T1 and T2 can enter at the same time, because they are using different locks.
----------------
Same lock: One thread enters → other thread waits.
Different locks: Both threads can enter at the same time.
